  "text": "Two more, quickly. Early stopping halts training once validation loss hasn't improved for a set number of epochs, the patience, and restores the best epoch's weights. Set the patience too short, and it stops on a plateau: this small network, with a patience of twenty, stops at epoch sixty-eight, though its best is epoch one sixty-eight. RMSE squares errors before averaging, so one big miss dominates it. MAE counts every unit of error alike, so one outlier moves it far less. Trained on squared error, a model predicts the mean; on absolute error, the median. R squared compares your squared error with always predicting the mean: one is perfect, zero is the mean's own score, and on new data it can go negative, which no share of variance explained could. You can now tell whether a model will generalize. Next: the models themselves, and how they learn.",
